{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793739647,"nonce":"lEHJ1GOG89HG9+P26qMFpcZM/ZQ=","title":"A coordinate inside an .evolves() formula keeps a phantom component axis","message":"In the native FEM evaluator a one-direction coordinate (`x` from `domain.variable(..., split=True)`) evaluates to `(n_quad, 1)`. In a weak term the axis is absorbed by the test function; in a state readout it rank-broadcasts against the `(n_quad,)` scalar history slice to `(n_quad, n_quad)`, and the march then fails rolling the buffer ('Cannot concatenate arrays with different numbers of dimensions').\n\nReproduction: `H.evolves(maximum(H.i(-1), psi + maximum(0.1 - abs(y - 0.5), 0.0)))` on a scalar history field. The same formula without the coordinate works. This is the history-seeded initial crack of a phase-field fracture model.\n\nExpected: a one-direction coordinate is `(n_quad,)`, like a scalar trial since the earlier squeeze of its phantom axis.","files":null}]}